01:32:56,864 --> 01:32:58,666
[SPEAKER_04]: Prosperity does not mean happiness.

1058
01:33:01,008 --> 01:33:04,570
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen you walk the hills.

1059
01:33:04,610 --> 01:33:05,871
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen you walk mountains.

1060
01:33:06,992 --> 01:33:10,915
[SPEAKER_01]: You seem to be very outdoorsy, kind of very active.

1061
01:33:14,648 --> 01:33:25,679
[SPEAKER_01]: I was just wondering, while you do, while you're on your hikes, on your walks, how do you, what do you, what do you do?

1062
01:33:25,899 --> 01:33:32,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you, are you mindful of your body during that walk?

1063
01:33:32,986 --> 01:33:35,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you kind of make a practice out of it?

1064
01:33:37,865 --> 01:33:45,889
[SPEAKER_04]: No, I tend to walk fast, so basically the breathing becomes very hard, and you feel like you're dying, and then the mind doesn't move in the face of that.

1065
01:33:48,510 --> 01:33:50,951
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's my hiking practice and trekking practice.

1066
01:33:52,072 --> 01:33:57,594
[SPEAKER_04]: Basically, expose yourself to the elements, so the mind doesn't move in those extreme circumstances.

1067
01:34:00,816 --> 01:34:05,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we do a bit of sports ourselves, but yeah, so...

1068
01:34:08,595 --> 01:34:18,575
[SPEAKER_01]: I was wondering how, yeah, what kind of things to incorporate while, you know, when you're on a longer bike ride, it gets pretty boring.

1069
01:34:18,635 --> 01:34:19,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's a nice time.

1070
01:34:20,672 --> 01:34:21,473
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I'm not...

1071
01:34:21,553 --> 01:34:25,416
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not doing it, like, for... for... in that sense, like, for some pleasure or something.

1072
01:34:25,436 --> 01:34:35,966
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, especially in Sri Lanka, when I used to go around the jungles on my own, the whole point was go through the difficult terrains that I know I cannot turn back and go back to the comfort.

1073
01:34:36,587 --> 01:34:39,930
[SPEAKER_04]: Once you cross that point, it will be harder to turn back than to keep going forward.

1074
01:34:40,450 --> 01:34:45,155
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you just have to endure it until it ends without your mind.

1075
01:34:45,748 --> 01:34:47,029
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, see, that's it.

1076
01:34:47,069 --> 01:34:52,174
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind wants to panic, the mind wants to move, the mind wants to change, the mind wants to deal with the circumstances, but you don't.

1077
01:34:52,214 --> 01:34:52,954
[SPEAKER_04]: You just endure it.

1078
01:34:54,195 --> 01:34:54,796
[SPEAKER_04]: Physically.

1079
01:34:54,816 --> 01:34:58,319
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's that purification that what I was talking about, enduring the elements.

1080
01:34:59,460 --> 01:35:00,481
[SPEAKER_04]: Enduring discomfort.

1081
01:35:01,722 --> 01:35:04,784
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not some great practice that you kind of need to predetermine.

1082
01:35:04,824 --> 01:35:07,907
[SPEAKER_04]: You just need to expose yourself to it and see how much your mind moves.

1083
01:35:08,788 --> 01:35:11,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Obviously, I remember in the beginning it was moving much more.

1084
01:35:11,210 --> 01:35:13,732
[SPEAKER_04]: Just the thought of doing that was moving me.

1085
01:35:15,063 --> 01:35:18,044
[SPEAKER_04]: Years ago.

1086
01:35:18,124 --> 01:35:18,824
[SPEAKER_04]: And you see that.

1087
01:35:18,884 --> 01:35:31,868
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, the thought of this comfort, physical rain, cold, you know, being stuck in the jungle and the vines and everything, leeches, you know, I can't be bothered.

1088
01:35:31,888 --> 01:35:34,929
[SPEAKER_04]: And, again, you don't do it blindly or that's why I just do it.

1089
01:35:34,969 --> 01:35:35,169
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

1090
01:35:35,229 --> 01:35:38,930
[SPEAKER_04]: Do it so to be bothered, so to not be moved by the physical body.

1091
01:35:39,411 --> 01:35:40,251
[SPEAKER_04]: Because what do you think?

1092
01:35:40,331 --> 01:35:43,592
[SPEAKER_04]: How will the illness feel when the body starts breaking apart?

1093
01:35:45,143 --> 01:35:50,185
[SPEAKER_04]: Joints aching, head hurting, eyes cannot see, you can barely breathe.

1094
01:35:50,245 --> 01:35:51,706
[SPEAKER_04]: It's gonna feel exactly like that.

1095
01:35:53,346 --> 01:35:56,827
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the first thing you get people who are hiking, is they become very grumpy.

1096
01:35:59,408 --> 01:36:05,451
[SPEAKER_04]: They start arguing with each other as well, because they start lashing out, because it starts building up just basic discomfort.

1097
01:36:06,811 --> 01:36:08,472
[SPEAKER_04]: And most people are sheltered from it.

1098
01:36:11,413 --> 01:36:13,554
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's why I do it, so you don't become complacent.

1099
01:36:13,614 --> 01:36:13,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry?

1100
01:36:15,436 --> 01:36:18,620
[SPEAKER_01]: It's wonderful because I'm always trying to avoid the discomfort, right?

1101
01:36:18,640 --> 01:36:20,001
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to go on that bike ride.

1102
01:36:20,041 --> 01:36:22,104
[SPEAKER_01]: I know it's going to take a very long time.

1103
01:36:22,124 --> 01:36:23,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be very dangerous.

1104
01:36:23,525 --> 01:36:26,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be uphill and whatever.

1105
01:36:26,268 --> 01:36:30,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when I do it, I really enjoy that feeling, exactly what you're describing.

1106
01:36:30,753 --> 01:36:32,735
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you can avoid the danger parts.

1107
01:36:33,296 --> 01:36:35,899
[SPEAKER_04]: There's no need to be, like, life-threatening or something.

1108
01:36:36,732 --> 01:36:50,597
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, you do that endurance and, exactly, yeah, once the mind stops moving in the face of that, it's not going to move into anything that's inferior to that.

1109
01:36:50,617 --> 01:36:51,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

1110
01:36:51,057 --> 01:36:54,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can incorporate dhamma into many things.

1111
01:36:54,918 --> 01:36:57,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I do not need the time slot after all.

1112
01:36:57,579 --> 01:36:58,179
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

1113
01:36:58,219 --> 01:36:59,000
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

1114
01:36:59,100 --> 01:36:59,520
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

1115
01:37:02,241 --> 01:37:03,501
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry, did you have a question as well?

1116
01:37:03,521 --> 01:37:03,821
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

1117
01:37:04,021 --> 01:37:05,162
[SPEAKER_04]: We have a question here as well.

1118
01:37:07,246 --> 01:37:17,393
[SPEAKER_03]: So, just to check if I understand you correctly with this not wanting to have panic and other stuff.

1119
01:37:17,674 --> 01:37:30,483
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it seems to me that on the one level, it seems to me that it is sort of understandable that I don't want them, because I don't want them

1120
01:37:31,517 --> 01:37:34,979
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I want to be, sort of, enlightened.

1121
01:37:35,620 --> 01:37:39,963
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'd say...

1122
01:37:40,183 --> 01:37:42,765
[SPEAKER_03]: So, not wanting...

1123
01:37:42,805 --> 01:37:58,495
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I'd say, perceiving them as a problem, maybe not so bad, so to say, because... because this makes you aware that you are not enlightened now, and...

1124
01:38:02,055 --> 01:38:26,859
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and this also can be then a way to soothe this discomfort, soothe this desire to get rid of them, because once you remind yourself that it is sort of normal that you experience these feelings, because you're not enlightened, this is already sort of, well,

1125
01:38:27,898 --> 01:38:30,361
[SPEAKER_03]: I see what you mean, but no, no, they're not a problem.

1126
01:38:40,941 --> 01:38:44,022
[SPEAKER_04]: See, you saying that they're the problem means saying they're the suffering.

1127
01:38:44,282 --> 01:38:46,123
[SPEAKER_04]: But suffering is not in the phenomena.

1128
01:38:46,143 --> 01:38:46,943
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not in dhammas.

1129
01:38:46,963 --> 01:38:47,844
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not in the things.

1130
01:38:48,184 --> 01:38:49,965
[SPEAKER_04]: It's in your craving in regard to them.

1131
01:38:50,085 --> 01:38:51,386
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the first noble truth.

1132
01:38:51,986 --> 01:38:56,488
[SPEAKER_04]: So, ironically enough, yes, enlightenment will make those things not be there.

1133
01:38:56,588 --> 01:38:57,929
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because they're not there.

1134
01:38:58,129 --> 01:39:00,290
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because you have no craving in regard to them.

1135
01:39:01,170 --> 01:39:05,092
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the noble truth of craving is not... there is no panic attack manifesting.

1136
01:39:05,112 --> 01:39:07,173
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no discomfort manifesting.

1137
01:39:07,870 --> 01:39:12,994
[SPEAKER_04]: The noble truth of craving is, there is no resistance in discomfort left whatsoever, ever again.

1138
01:39:14,655 --> 01:39:18,778
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the root of suffering is taṇha, not the world.

1139
01:39:19,239 --> 01:39:21,400
[SPEAKER_04]: Things remain standing in the world, coming...

1140
01:39:21,741 --> 01:39:28,626
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, I'm sure Angulimala, when, after his arahantship, when those kids threw stones and split his head open,

1141
01:39:29,462 --> 01:39:31,803
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm sure he felt the discomfort on account of it.

1142
01:39:32,324 --> 01:39:35,966
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, the Buddha wouldn't have come to him and said, bear it, Brahmin, bear it.

1143
01:39:36,166 --> 01:39:41,529
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, the results, you know, if you didn't become an Arahant, would have been far worse than what you're experiencing now.

1144
01:39:42,512 --> 01:39:44,033
[SPEAKER_04]: But he had no craving left.

1145
01:39:44,673 --> 01:39:45,333
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's why it's...

1146
01:39:45,393 --> 01:39:52,056
[SPEAKER_04]: I know what you mean, like, yeah, because you could then just say, okay, so let's not make a problem out of anything and forget that you're not enlightened.

1147
01:39:52,676 --> 01:39:57,038
[SPEAKER_04]: But I think people, they're gripped by panic attacks, they don't need reminders that they're not enlightened.

1148
01:39:57,638 --> 01:39:59,298
[SPEAKER_04]: They know that very well and they feel it.

1149
01:39:59,959 --> 01:40:02,820
[SPEAKER_04]: They need a reminder to see where the line of the problem is.

1150
01:40:03,520 --> 01:40:04,621
[SPEAKER_04]: And panic is not that.

1151
01:40:05,142 --> 01:40:15,291
[SPEAKER_04]: Your resistance to it is, which is there because of your equal non-resistance to pleasure, when it's there beforehand or after.

1152
01:40:17,292 --> 01:40:18,273
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1153
01:40:19,615 --> 01:40:19,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Go.

1154
01:40:20,015 --> 01:40:20,315
[SPEAKER_03]: Polo.

1155
01:40:20,595 --> 01:40:20,776
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1156
01:40:21,096 --> 01:40:22,517
[SPEAKER_03]: So, another thing.

1157
01:40:22,877 --> 01:40:28,723
[SPEAKER_03]: So, let's say, in the in the broken context, so many of us find ourselves in

1158
01:40:29,495 --> 01:40:36,819
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, in the situations where non-acting is, so to say, dangerous to career.

1159
01:40:37,339 --> 01:40:42,961
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and for me, it basically means that you just need to give up those, to leave those contexts.

1160
01:40:43,001 --> 01:40:43,882
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure.

1161
01:40:44,042 --> 01:40:47,984
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's, yeah, that's the conflict that you, in your setting, need to resolve with yourself.

1162
01:40:49,004 --> 01:40:53,807
[SPEAKER_04]: Practice of dhamma, yeah, you cannot be working and being a layman and fulfilling the practice of dhamma.

1163
01:40:54,087 --> 01:40:55,707
[SPEAKER_04]: You can only do in a limited manner.

1164
01:40:56,888 --> 01:40:58,369
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, yeah, it is mutually exclusive.

1165
01:40:58,389 --> 01:40:58,749
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

1166
01:40:59,793 --> 01:41:04,435
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the more you do it, the more you will be, sort of, withdrawing yourself from all the other things you need to do.

1167
01:41:04,855 --> 01:41:05,995
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can't have it both ways.

1168
01:41:08,556 --> 01:41:08,756
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1169
01:41:09,597 --> 01:41:12,718
[SPEAKER_03]: And, eh... so, maybe, eh... last question on my side.

1170
01:41:13,298 --> 01:41:14,198
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh...

1171
01:41:14,759 --> 01:41:23,862
[SPEAKER_03]: I... after I started doing, eh... well, I could... well... maybe one could call it, like, this practice of non-activity, just, I don't know, to sit down and

1172
01:41:24,553 --> 01:41:36,626
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, to sit for, let's say, maybe an hour, maybe two hours, to see how the mind sort of comes down in it, I found it very, very pleasant.

1173
01:41:38,147 --> 01:41:39,809
[SPEAKER_03]: And then I said to myself,

1174
01:41:40,569 --> 01:41:47,475
[SPEAKER_03]: I started asking myself, so, is this pleasure now a problem that I'm sort of enjoying, basically, like this dhamma?

1175
01:41:47,535 --> 01:41:53,220
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know how much of it is maybe essential pleasure, because I sort of allow my body to rest.

1176
01:41:53,901 --> 01:41:59,666
[SPEAKER_03]: So, my question is, is it a problem that you're experiencing pleasure on account of your...

1177
01:42:01,287 --> 01:42:02,308
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, what kind of pleasure?

1178
01:42:02,348 --> 01:42:03,388
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, contentment?

1179
01:42:03,468 --> 01:42:04,029
[SPEAKER_03]: Mental joy?

1180
01:42:04,389 --> 01:42:05,509
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, something like this, yeah.

1181
01:42:05,530 --> 01:42:15,515
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're just... the feeling that you're... Do you become unaware of yourself on account of that pleasure?

1182
01:42:15,695 --> 01:42:16,696
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you become distracted?

1183
01:42:16,716 --> 01:42:21,739
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you feel like you woke up when that pleasure ceases or you've remained fully mindful while the pleasure is there?

1184
01:42:25,628 --> 01:42:29,812
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, um, I think I remain mindful of it, yeah, so it's... Yeah, so keep an eye on that.

1185
01:42:29,832 --> 01:42:37,658
[SPEAKER_04]: For as long as you remain mindful, and it's clearly the result of you non-engaging with your senses, it's not the pleasure you should be overly concerned with.

1186
01:42:38,719 --> 01:42:48,948
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but I'm asking myself, so maybe it's like, maybe it's because I'm sitting in a chair and this is pleasant, so... Sure, it might be, but that's not the central pleasure.

1187
01:42:49,608 --> 01:42:53,792
[SPEAKER_04]: That's not the pleasure that's inducive of lust, thirst, and loss of perspective.

1188
01:42:54,738 --> 01:43:01,446
[SPEAKER_04]: But that is certainly the mind of doubt that's wanting you... wanting to sort of, you know, move you away from that experience of non-activity.

1189
01:43:01,647 --> 01:43:02,988
[SPEAKER_04]: One... it wants to make you act.

1190
01:43:04,190 --> 01:43:08,275
[SPEAKER_04]: Hey, stop doing this, because it actually works against the Mahārāja's order.

1191
01:43:09,655 --> 01:43:10,615
[SPEAKER_04]: Start thinking about it.

1192
01:43:10,655 --> 01:43:11,256
[SPEAKER_04]: Start worrying.

1193
01:43:11,296 --> 01:43:13,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Start undermining it with your... Yeah.

1194
01:43:13,676 --> 01:43:16,197
[SPEAKER_04]: Mind comes up with the thought, is this... You check it.

1195
01:43:16,377 --> 01:43:17,237
[SPEAKER_04]: Mindfulness is there.

1196
01:43:17,538 --> 01:43:19,118
[SPEAKER_04]: You are not actually engaging with anything.

1197
01:43:19,618 --> 01:43:19,758
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

1198
01:43:19,778 --> 01:43:20,879
[SPEAKER_04]: The body feels agreeable.

1199
01:43:20,899 --> 01:43:22,319
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, the Buddha ate the milk rice.

1200
01:43:23,599 --> 01:43:27,201
[SPEAKER_04]: And indeed, those ascetics thought, look, he went back.

1201
01:43:27,361 --> 01:43:27,961
[SPEAKER_04]: He lost it.

1202
01:43:28,701 --> 01:43:30,342
[SPEAKER_04]: He uses the Atman's sensuality.

1203
01:43:31,302 --> 01:43:34,623
[SPEAKER_04]: So, to some extent, yeah, you must not torture the body all the time.

1204
01:43:36,244 --> 01:43:38,927
[SPEAKER_04]: But, equally, what I was talking about is this hiking thing.

1205
01:43:38,967 --> 01:43:44,814
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, most people would not be asking that question to themselves, which you're asking.

1206
01:43:45,274 --> 01:43:54,445
[SPEAKER_04]: They would probably be on the level of, well, I don't want to deal with the discomfort, even of the basic elemental kind, unless I really have to.

1207
01:43:56,153 --> 01:44:03,357
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's then their practice, is to know, actually, let me see how much will my mind move on account of things that are not threatening to my life.

1208
01:44:03,917 --> 01:44:05,558
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you know, I remember leeches in Sri Lanka.

1209
01:44:05,578 --> 01:44:07,339
[SPEAKER_04]: People would lose their mind on account of it.

1210
01:44:08,259 --> 01:44:09,099
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, it's nothing.

1211
01:44:09,119 --> 01:44:10,560
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't get diseases from them.

1212
01:44:10,640 --> 01:44:12,121
[SPEAKER_04]: They, you know, there's no permanent damage.

1213
01:44:12,181 --> 01:44:15,082
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just unpleasant and psychologically uncomfortable.

1214
01:44:16,203 --> 01:44:20,305
[SPEAKER_04]: And people will just, like, forsake any sense of reason just on account of those things.

1215
01:44:20,385 --> 01:44:22,066
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, you should not be allowing that to yourself.

1216
01:44:22,146 --> 01:44:24,427
[SPEAKER_04]: Like monks, if you want to practice and so on.

1217
01:44:25,785 --> 01:44:34,068
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, yeah, how do you think it's going to feel when the body starts breaking apart and so on, when these little external discomforts are throwing you left and right?

1218
01:44:35,288 --> 01:44:42,990
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, then you have the other extreme where people can just go and start torturing for the sake of, like, self-inflicting pain because they don't know where the line is.

1219
01:44:44,631 --> 01:44:45,531
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's where the line is.

1220
01:44:47,132 --> 01:44:51,833
[SPEAKER_04]: Find the non-activity, if it's agreeable, don't lose mindfulness on account of it, and that's it.

1221
01:44:53,312 --> 01:44:53,993
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't be doing it.

1222
01:44:54,054 --> 01:44:59,324
[SPEAKER_04]: You will have to get up and do something at some point, so... And then, when you go back, if it happens the same, same.

1223
01:44:59,645 --> 01:45:00,126
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter.

1224
01:45:11,560 --> 01:45:24,431
[SPEAKER_02]: I understand everyone perceives these things differently, but for myself, I also, let's say, saw it in this sense, let's... endurance sports, let's say, as... okay, it's first, like...

1225
01:45:24,651 --> 01:45:38,442
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, first, yeah, that's... body training, in a way, and the other aspect was also exposing the mind to this uncomfortable sensation, and enduring it.

1226
01:45:39,162 --> 01:45:39,443
[SPEAKER_02]: But...

1227
01:45:41,638 --> 01:45:44,361
[SPEAKER_02]: How to say?

1228
01:45:44,581 --> 01:45:55,051
[SPEAKER_02]: It can definitely strengthen the mind, but I think at some point or... but that's probably like on a case-to-case basis.

1229
01:45:56,440 --> 01:45:58,881
[SPEAKER_02]: It can also be a distraction from neutral thinking, I think.

1230
01:45:58,901 --> 01:45:59,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, of course, sure.

1231
01:45:59,982 --> 01:46:02,283
[SPEAKER_04]: So, for me, it was then... No, that's what I mean.

1232
01:46:02,343 --> 01:46:06,786
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, as I said before, the whole point is... Well, that's, yeah, that's the practice.

1233
01:46:07,406 --> 01:46:10,088
[SPEAKER_04]: Is to be able to judge where your motivation is rooted.

1234
01:46:10,308 --> 01:46:11,989
[SPEAKER_04]: What I said earlier, half an hour ago.

1235
01:46:12,710 --> 01:46:14,951
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't just say, ah, so physicality is always good.

1236
01:46:15,611 --> 01:46:16,812
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, oh, that's always bad.

1237
01:46:17,198 --> 01:46:19,221
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, but being aware of, like, okay, it's mental.

1238
01:46:19,241 --> 01:46:20,182
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you doing it?

1239
01:46:20,222 --> 01:46:21,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you doing it for?

1240
01:46:21,403 --> 01:46:21,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

1241
01:46:21,904 --> 01:46:30,094
[SPEAKER_04]: So, halfway through your doing of your physical activity for strengthening your body and conditioning your mind, you ask yourself, am I still doing it for the same reasons?

1242
01:46:30,795 --> 01:46:33,679
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you realize, oh, no, I'm doing it because I really want to be that guy.

1243
01:46:34,845 --> 01:46:35,586
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you stop it.

1244
01:46:35,646 --> 01:46:39,949
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you clearly know that it's the reasons have changed, because reasons change, intentions change.

1245
01:46:40,549 --> 01:46:44,171
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's when you don't follow through with the actions on account, because this is always good.

1246
01:46:44,612 --> 01:46:46,993
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what it means, like, you take responsibility for your action.

1247
01:46:47,013 --> 01:46:48,394
[SPEAKER_04]: Not like, you never do that.

1248
01:46:48,434 --> 01:46:53,037
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, you never lose the sight of why this is done in the first place.

1249
01:46:53,057 --> 01:46:57,881
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but in the sense that, like, this unneutral feeling is on the more subtle level.

1250
01:46:58,481 --> 01:47:03,664
[SPEAKER_02]: And trying to escape it through an excuse of training the mind might be tricky.

1251
01:47:03,724 --> 01:47:08,466
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, again, you know, sometimes it can happen, but if you keep doing it, it will become apparent.

1252
01:47:09,186 --> 01:47:11,047
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you'll see if you're doing it for that reason.

1253
01:47:12,888 --> 01:47:14,069
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's equal.

1254
01:47:14,169 --> 01:47:17,871
[SPEAKER_04]: Again, like, how do you think when back in the in the day the Buddhists, the monks,

1255
01:47:18,651 --> 01:47:29,775
[SPEAKER_04]: lived around India, where they would go on foot, carry all their possessions that they had, every time, for days, just to meet the Buddha and talk to him for five minutes, and then wander around...

1256
01:47:29,795 --> 01:47:31,516
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, you... have you been in Bihar?

1257
01:47:31,536 --> 01:47:37,758
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know if you've been to India, but it's not a place you want to wander around now, and I can only imagine back then how it was.

1258
01:47:38,893 --> 01:47:44,278
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, today probably soldiers wouldn't be able to do that, physically, without all the gear and equipment and help.

1259
01:47:44,578 --> 01:47:46,139
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, that was like a normal life, you know.

1260
01:47:46,179 --> 01:47:52,405
[SPEAKER_04]: The woman goes on a journey with her two kids across the entire northern India to visit her family.

1261
01:47:52,705 --> 01:47:54,847
[SPEAKER_04]: And, you know, get attacked by jackals and so on.

1262
01:47:55,147 --> 01:47:56,288
[SPEAKER_04]: That was like a normal thing.

1263
01:47:57,289 --> 01:47:58,430
[SPEAKER_04]: So, people were tough.

1264
01:47:59,769 --> 01:48:03,971
[SPEAKER_04]: And today, how many people can say they can do it?

1265
01:48:04,391 --> 01:48:11,174
[SPEAKER_04]: Again, why you want to do it is what matters, but don't ignore the fact that some extent of it needs to be done, still.

1266
01:48:12,195 --> 01:48:16,237
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that whole... was it the Vāsava Sutta?

1267
01:48:16,277 --> 01:48:23,900
[SPEAKER_04]: The whole, like, set of taints, āsavas, to be abandoned by enduring heat, cold, hunger, exposure, and so on.

